## Proof

Let $X = M_n(\mathbb{R})$ and let $I$ denote the identity matrix. We work with any submultiplicative matrix norm $\|\cdot\|$ (all such norms on the finite-dimensional space $M_n(\mathbb{R})$ are equivalent, so the topological notions below are norm-independent).

### Step 1. Defining the neighborhoods and the square-root map

Set
$$V := \{A \in M_n(\mathbb{R}) : \|A - I\| < 1\},$$
an open neighborhood of $I$. For $A \in V$ write $A = I + H$ with $\|H\| < 1$. Consider the binomial series
$$(I+H)^{1/2} := \sum_{k=0}^{\infty} \binom{1/2}{k} H^k, \qquad \binom{1/2}{k} = \frac{\tfrac12(\tfrac12-1)\cdots(\tfrac12-k+1)}{k!}.$$

Because $\|H\|<1$, this series converges absolutely in $M_n(\mathbb{R})$ (its scalar majorant $\sum |\binom{1/2}{k}|\,\|H\|^k$ converges for $\|H\|<1$, the radius of convergence of $(1+x)^{1/2}$). Denote its sum by $B(H)$. Since matrix multiplication is continuous and the partial sums are polynomials in $H$, we may square the series term-by-term (Cauchy product), and the classical scalar identity
$$\Bigl(\sum_{k\ge 0}\tbinom{1/2}{k}x^k\Bigr)^2 = 1+x \qquad (|x|<1)$$
lifts coefficient-by-coefficient to matrices, giving
$$B(H)^2 = I + H = A.$$

Thus every $A \in V$ admits a square root $B(H)$ defined by this convergent series. Moreover $B(0)=I$, so $\phi(I)=I$. Define
$$\phi: V \to M_n(\mathbb{R}), \qquad \phi(A) := \phi(I+H) := B(H) = \sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\tbinom{1/2}{k}H^k,$$
and set $U := \phi(V)$. Since $\phi$ is continuous and $V$ is open with $I\in V$, $U$ is an open neighborhood of $I=\phi(I)$ (continuity gives $\phi(V)\supseteq$ an open ball around $I$; more directly, $\phi$ is a local diffeomorphism at $I$ as shown below, so $U$ is open). Hence $U,V$ are open neighborhoods of $I$ and $\phi:V\to U$ is well defined.

### Step 2. Differentiability at $I$ and computation of the derivative

The series defining $\phi$ is a convergent power series in $H$ on the open ball $\|H\|<1$. A power series is term-by-term differentiable inside its radius of convergence: each coefficient map $H\mapsto H^k$ is a homogeneous polynomial of degree $k$ in the entries of $H$, hence smooth, and the derived series converges uniformly on every smaller ball $\|H\|\le r<1$. Therefore $\phi$ is differentiable (in fact real-analytic) at every point of $V$, in particular at $I$.

Extracting the linear term of the series at $H=0$:
$$\phi(I+H) = \binom{1/2}{0}I + \binom{1/2}{1}H + \binom{1/2}{2}H^2 + \cdots = I + \tfrac12 H + O(\|H\|^2).$$

Since $\phi(I)=I$, subtracting gives
$$\phi(I+H) - \phi(I) = \tfrac12 H + O(\|H\|^2).$$

By definition of the Fréchet derivative, the linear part in $H$ is $d\phi(I)(H)$. Therefore, writing $T$ for an arbitrary tangent vector at $I$ (i.e. an arbitrary element of $M_n(\mathbb{R})$, with $H = A - I = T$ when linearizing),

$$\boxed{\,d\phi(I)(T) = \tfrac12\,T\,} \qquad \text{for all } T \in M_n(\mathbb{R}).$$

### Step 3. Verification

As a consistency check, let $B = I + \tfrac12 T + O(\|T\|^2)$. Then
$$B^2 = \Bigl(I+\tfrac12 T\Bigr)^2 + O(\|T\|^2) = I + T + \tfrac14 T^2 + O(\|T\|^2) = I + T + O(\|T\|^2),$$
which agrees with $A = I+T$ to first order, confirming that the linearization of the implicit equation $B^2 = A$ at $(B,A)=(I,I)$ is $2\,dB = dA$, i.e. $dB = \tfrac12\,dA$. This matches $d\phi(I)(T)=\tfrac12 T$ and is consistent with the inverse-function-theorem viewpoint (the map $B\mapsto B^2$ has derivative $T\mapsto 2T$ at $I$, which is invertible, giving a local inverse whose derivative is $T\mapsto \tfrac12 T$).

### Conclusion

Such neighborhoods exist: take $V=\{A:\|A-I\|<1\}$ and $U=\phi(V)$, with $\phi$ defined by the convergent binomial series $(I+H)^{1/2}=\sum_{k\ge0}\binom{1/2}{k}H^k$. The map $\phi$ is real-analytic on $V$, hence differentiable at $I$, and its derivative at the identity is

$$d\phi(I)(T) = \tfrac{1}{2}\,T, \qquad T\in M_n(\mathbb{R}).$$

